    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "## Summary\nOn Windows, AO injects Codex hook commands that begin with a quoted absolute path, but no PowerShell call operator. Codex executes these command hooks through PowerShell, so `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit`, and `Stop` fail before `ao.exe` starts.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Use the AO desktop install on Windows and start a Codex session.\n2. AO emits a hook command equivalent to:\n   ```text\n   \"<AO executable path>\" hooks codex stop\n   ```\n3. Run that command in PowerShell.\n\n### Actual result\nPowerShell returns:\n```text\nUnexpected token 'hooks' in expression or statement.\n```\nThe hook exits with code 1. The same generated form is used for `session-start` and `user-prompt-submit`.\n\n### Expected result\nThe command should execute the binary and return its status.\n\n## Root cause\nIn `backend/internal/adapters/agent/codex/hooks.go`, `shellQuoteHookExecutable` returns `\"<path>\"` on Windows. In PowerShell that is a string expression, not an invocation.\n\n## Proposed fix\nGenerate the Windows prefix as:\n```text\n& \"<AO executable path>\"\n```\nso the final command is:\n```text\n& \"<AO executable path>\" hooks codex stop\n```\nAlternatively, invoke through `cmd.exe /d /s /c` with correct quoting.\n\n## Verification\nThe literal generated command reproducibly exits 1 in PowerShell. Adding `&` invokes the same `ao hooks codex stop` command successfully with exit 0. No credentials, payloads, or user content are required to reproduce.\r\n",
